 ## Overview
-TSDF Volume Integration is a method for reconstructing a 3D surface from a set of RGB-D images by accumulating truncated signed distance fields into a voxel grid. It solves the problem of noisy or incomplete depth data by weighting each voxel's distance value by the confidence of the measurement, effectively filtering out outliers and filling gaps. It differs from algebraic methods (like MIP or standard volume rendering) by explicitly modeling surface proximity via a truncation function, and from pure mesh extraction techniques by operating in a volumetric space before marching cubes.
+TSDF Volume Integration builds on [MeshIntegrator Integration](3630-meshintegrator-integration.md) to write a triangle mesh's truncated distance field into a `VoxelBlockGrid`'s `tsdf` and `weight` attributes [Parent]. It accumulates truncated signed distance fields from multiple RGB-D views into a voxel grid to reconstruct a 3D surface [S1, unofficial]. It differs from algebraic volume rendering methods by explicitly modeling surface proximity via a truncation function, and from pure mesh extraction techniques by operating in a volumetric space before surface reconstruction [S1, unofficial].
 
